  "lineage": "Renaissance secular polyphonic vocal genre developed in sixteenth-century Italy through Jacques Arcadelt, Adrian Willaert, and culminating in Carlo Gesualdo (1566-1613) and Claudio Monteverdi (1567-1643). Set Italian secular poetry in elaborate four-to-six-voice polyphonic textures with characteristic word-painting and increasingly chromatic harmonic vocabulary (especially in Gesualdo). The English madrigal school (Thomas Morley, John Wilbye, Thomas Weelkes) flourished briefly in 1580s-1610s as an Italian-derived English adaptation. Direct precursor to Baroque secular cantata and opera.",
